<figure class="visual">
        <figcaption class="visual-cap"><span class="label">What failure changes</span><h3>What happens when a target becomes unhealthy?</h3><p>Read across each row to see who detects failure, where new traffic goes, what happens to existing traffic, and why an all-unhealthy fail-open state is not recovery.</p></figcaption>
        <div class="health-grid" role="table" aria-label="Health behavior by service">
          <div class="health-row head" role="row"><div role="columnheader">Layer</div><div role="columnheader">Health boundary</div><div role="columnheader">New traffic</div><div role="columnheader">Existing traffic</div><div role="columnheader">Every candidate unhealthy</div></div>
          <div class="health-row" role="row"><div class="service" data-label="Layer" role="rowheader">ALB</div><div data-label="Health boundary" role="cell">Target in target group<span class="observe">Observe: reason codes, target metrics</span></div><div data-label="New traffic" role="cell">Select another healthy target</div><div data-label="Existing traffic" role="cell">Depends on protocol and draining context</div><div data-label="Every candidate unhealthy" role="cell"><span class="fail-open"><strong>FAIL OPEN</strong>Routes to all targets in the affected group</span></div></div>
          <div class="health-row" role="row"><div class="service" data-label="Layer" role="rowheader">NLB</div><div data-label="Health boundary" role="cell">Target plus zonal threshold<span class="observe">Observe: zonal health, target metrics</span></div><div data-label="New traffic" role="cell">Avoid target; zonal DNS/routing can change</div><div data-label="Existing traffic" role="cell">Do not assume migration; reset/drain varies</div><div data-label="Every candidate unhealthy" role="cell"><span class="fail-open"><strong>FAIL OPEN</strong>Can route to unhealthy registered targets</span></div></div>
          <div class="health-row" role="row"><div class="service" data-label="Layer" role="rowheader">GWLB</div><div data-label="Health boundary" role="cell">Appliance target<span class="observe">Observe: target health, packet loss</span></div><div data-label="New traffic" role="cell">Select a healthy appliance</div><div data-label="Existing traffic" role="cell">Default keeps its appliance; failover is configurable</div><div data-label="Every candidate unhealthy" role="cell"><span class="fail-open"><strong>FAIL OPEN</strong>Picks an unhealthy target; traffic may drop</span></div></div>
          <div class="health-row" role="row"><div class="service" data-label="Layer" role="rowheader">Global Accelerator</div><div data-label="Health boundary" role="cell">Standard endpoint / endpoint group<span class="observe">Observe: endpoint and app health</span></div><div data-label="New traffic" role="cell">Select another healthy endpoint</div><div data-label="Existing traffic" role="cell">Dial changes do not terminate it</div><div data-label="Every candidate unhealthy" role="cell"><span class="fail-open"><strong>FAIL OPEN</strong>Search can end at a random endpoint in closest group</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <details><summary>Why “fail open” is not recovery</summary><p>The service stops excluding every failed candidate because exclusion would leave no route. The selected target can still reject, time out, or drop traffic. Alert on the original health loss and the resulting request outcome.</p></details>
        <div class="takeaway"><strong>Separate new from existing traffic.</strong> A health or dial change often affects the next selection; it does not teleport an established connection to a new target.</div>
      </figure>
